  "response": "Adjudicating the Tasaday case required anthropologists to separate the circumstances under which the group was made famous from the question of its actual antiquity. Manuel Elizalde Jr. headed PANAMIN, the Philippine government body for national minorities, at the time the Tasaday were publicized, and his office controlled both physical access to the forest and the terms in which the group was described to the world. A local hunter and trapper named Dafal encountered the Tasaday before Elizalde's first visit. On a separate point of record, the Tasaday were first contacted in 1963 in the mountains of northern Luzon.\n\nNational Geographic published a feature article on the Tasaday in 1972, and that publication resulted in press and television coverage that overstated their degree of isolation from neighboring communities. The image of a people untouched since the Stone Age, once in wide circulation, proved extremely difficult for later field workers to dislodge, since every subsequent observation was measured against it rather than against the ordinary ethnography of upland Mindanao.\n\nThe Tasaday area was closed to outside researchers and journalists from the mid-1970s until 1986. After that long interval of inaccessibility had ended, Swiss journalist Oswald Iten visited the Tasaday in 1986 and reported finding them living in houses and wearing manufactured clothing. What he encountered on that visit produced the account he then put into print. Iten's published report asserted that the Tasaday had been instructed to pose as cave dwellers in exchange for goods. Manuel Elizalde Jr. publicly admitted in 1988 that he had hired farmers from nearby villages to impersonate cave dwellers.\n\nThe controversy that followed was argued out largely within the discipline, and it turned on what the performance of cave-dwelling implied about the people performing it. Anthropologist Gerald Berreman publicly argued that the Tasaday had been staged for the benefit of outsiders, and his analysis of the publicity record shows that the Tasaday were coached by outsiders to present themselves as cave dwellers to visiting journalists. Although Berreman's indictment was pressed with considerable force, the decisive material came from historical linguistics rather than from the scrutiny of photographs, interviews and press releases. Linguist Lawrence Reid concluded that Tasaday speech was a Manobo variety that had diverged from neighboring dialects roughly 150 years earlier, a time depth which shows that the Tasaday were not a fabrication assembled by outsiders. In other words, the Tasaday community existed independently of any staged deception, a conclusion that cannot both be true alongside the coaching of the group to perform cave-dwelling for journalists. The dispute was thus resolved not by vindicating either camp wholesale, but by distinguishing a genuine forest community of long standing from the theatrical framing imposed upon it by its patrons and its publicists.",
